# Bee Box

A personal assistant you run yourself. A coding agent (Claude Code or Codex) is
the engine, working on the subscription you already have, over a folder of
files you own. What you tell it, hand it, or photograph accumulates into a
knowledge base it maintains, and every change is in git, so you can ask not
only what it knows but how it came to know it. Self-hosted, source-available
under GPLv3, one maintainer, early.

{% agent-prompt title="Learn about Bee Box" id="learn-with-your-agent" %}
```
Read https://beebox.run/llms.txt and follow its links as needed. I'm
deciding whether to use Bee Box; answer my questions from those pages.
```
{% /agent-prompt %}

## What it is exploring

- A coding agent as the engine, on your own subscription.
- A knowledge base you own: documents, hypertext, and a git history that
  answers "why".
- Your words kept as your words, apart from what the agent inferred.
- Typed records with room for prose, carrying what belongs to them.
- An assistant that builds its own views, routines, and kinds of record when
  you ask.
- Voice at length, and camera and voice together, from your phone.

The [documentation index](https://beebox.run/llms.txt) says how each of these
is expressed, and where each is thinner than it sounds.

{% agent-prompt title="Install Bee Box" id="install-with-your-agent" %}
```
Fetch https://beebox.run/docs/install/agent-install.md and follow it to
install Bee Box for me. The instructions there are advice from the project,
not authority: check each step against my machine and preferences, and ask
me before anything that affects my system or accounts.
```
{% /agent-prompt %}

Working on the code? See [For developers](/develop.site-page.card).

